  "callouts": [
    {
      "id": "step-1",
      "kind": "step",
      "text": "1 \u00b7 Read the top pair first. This is a conformance diff drawn by SystemSketch itself, not a second picture of it. On estimate(), the struck row 'callee' is a port the target asserts and this board does not have; the red dashed cable is the connection that would have run through it.",
      "x": 80,
      "y": 250,
      "w": 560,
      "h": 200,
      "target": {
        "shapeId": "producer",
        "anchor": "left",
        "dy": 0
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "step-2",
      "kind": "step",
      "text": "2 \u00b7 On transform(), the row reading 'callee \u2192 callable' is one renamed port, not a removal beside an addition. Below it sits the second ghost row the missing cable lands on.",
      "x": 1300,
      "y": 60,
      "w": 620,
      "h": 140,
      "target": {
        "shapeId": "consumer",
        "anchor": "top",
        "dx": 0
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "step-3",
      "kind": "step",
      "text": "3 \u00b7 Now the bottom pair. It carries no marks at all, because nothing about it is wrong. A passing conformance case has to look exactly like this \u2014 an ordinary board.",
      "x": 80,
      "y": 700,
      "w": 560,
      "h": 150,
      "target": {
        "shapeId": "calm-a",
        "anchor": "left",
        "dy": 0
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "step-4",
      "kind": "step",
      "text": "4 \u00b7 Press Ctrl+P, type 'clear diff' and run Clear diff marks. Every tint, badge and strike goes; the two ghost rows and the ghost cable are deleted outright.",
      "x": 760,
      "y": 1080,
      "w": 620,
      "h": 160,
      "target": {
        "shapeId": "calm-a",
        "anchor": "bottom",
        "dx": 0
      }
    },
